After exhaustive multi-pass review of every cell in the notebook with extensive edge case analysis, I confirm these 6 verified bugs are the complete list.

{"findings": [{"body": "Cell 6 uses `df_train.sample(frac=1)` without setting a `random_state` parameter. Combined with the positional split in Cell 22 (`train = df_train[:7000]; val = df_train[7000:]`), this means the train/val split changes on every run. This makes results non-reproducible and prevents fair comparison between runs. Fix: Add `random_state=42` to `df_train.sample(frac=1, random_state=42)`.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Non-deterministic train/validation split due to missing random_state in Cell 6"}, {"body": "In Cell 42, `validation_class_weights = torch.tensor(...)` creates a CPU tensor, which is passed to `torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=validation_class_weights)`. Inside the validation loop, batches and model outputs are moved to GPU (`batch = {k: v.to(device) for k, v in batch.items()}`). When `weighted_validation_loss(logits, batch['labels'])` is called, PyTorch raises `RuntimeError: Expected all tensors to be on the same device` because the weight tensor is on CPU while logits/labels are on GPU. This causes validation to crash whenever GPU is available. Fix: Add `.to(device)` to the weights: `validation_class_weights = torch.tensor(...).to(device)`.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Device mismatch causes RuntimeError on GPU in validation loss"}, {"body": "Cell 42 computes class weights from the validation set (`val['target'].value_counts()`) and creates a weighted CrossEntropyLoss used only for validation metrics. The training loop uses the model's default unweighted loss (`loss = outputs.loss`). This creates a mismatch: the model is trained with an unweighted objective while validation is measured with a weighted loss. Additionally, computing class weights from the validation set is unconventional — they should be computed from the training set. Fix: Compute class weights from `train['target']` and apply them to the training loss: `loss_fn = torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=training_class_weights.to(device))` then `loss = loss_fn(outputs.logits, batch['labels'])`.", "confidence": 0.88, "title": "Class imbalance weights computed from validation set and applied only to validation loss"}, {"body": "In Cell 16, `df_train['text_final'] = (df_train['text_cleaned']).map(str) + ' | ' + (df_train['keyword']).map(str)`. The `keyword` column in the nlp-getting-started dataset has many NaN values (~1/3 of rows). When `str(np.nan)` is called, it produces the string 'nan', which gets concatenated into the text. The tokenizer then treats 'nan' as a regular vocabulary word, introducing spurious tokens into the model input for all samples with missing keywords. Fix: Fill NaN values before mapping: `(df_train['keyword'].fillna('')).map(str)`.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "NaN keyword values produce spurious 'nan' tokens in model input"}, {"body": "In Cell 42, `metric = evaluate.load('accuracy')` creates an accuracy metric before the epoch loop. Inside the loop, `metric.add_batch(...)` is called for each validation batch, and `result = metric.compute()` is called at the end of each epoch. However, `metric.reset()` is never called between epochs. This means the accuracy metric accumulates across epochs: epoch 1's accuracy covers only epoch 1 data, but epoch 2's accuracy covers both epoch 1 and epoch 2 data combined. Fix: Call `metric.reset()` at the start of each epoch's validation loop.", "confidence": 0.92, "title": "Evaluation accuracy metric not reset between epochs - accumulates across epochs"}, {"body": "No `np.random.seed()`, `torch.manual_seed()`, or any random seed is set anywhere in the notebook. Combined with the missing seed in `df_train.sample(frac=1)` (Cell 6), the DataLoader shuffling (Cell 32), and the random weight initialization of the neural network, the entire training procedure is non-deterministic. Results will vary between runs. Fix: Add `random_seed = 42; np.random.seed(random_seed); torch.manual_seed(random_seed); torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(random_seed)` at the start of the notebook.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "No global random seeds anywhere in notebook - non-reproducible training"}]}


{"findings": [{"body": "Cell 6 uses `df_train.sample(frac=1)` without setting a `random_state` parameter. Combined with the positional split in Cell 22 (`train = df_train[:7000]; val = df_train[7000:]`), this means the train/val split changes on every run. This makes results non-reproducible and prevents fair comparison between runs. Fix: Add `random_state=42` to `df_train.sample(frac=1, random_state=42)`.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Non-deterministic train/validation split due to missing random_state in Cell 6"}, {"body": "In Cell 42, `validation_class_weights = torch.tensor(...)` creates a CPU tensor, which is passed to `torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=validation_class_weights)`. Inside the validation loop, batches and model outputs are moved to GPU (`batch = {k: v.to(device) for k, v in batch.items()}`). When `weighted_validation_loss(logits, batch['labels'])` is called, PyTorch raises `RuntimeError: Expected all tensors to be on the same device` because the weight tensor is on CPU while logits/labels are on GPU. This causes validation to crash whenever GPU is available. Fix: Add `.to(device)` to the weights: `validation_class_weights = torch.tensor(...).to(device)`.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Device mismatch causes RuntimeError on GPU in validation loss"}, {"body": "Cell 42 computes class weights from the validation set (`val['target'].value_counts()`) and creates a weighted CrossEntropyLoss used only for validation metrics. The training loop uses the model's default unweighted loss (`loss = outputs.loss`). This creates a mismatch: the model is trained with an unweighted objective while validation is measured with a weighted loss. Additionally, computing class weights from the validation set is unconventional — they should be computed from the training set. Fix: Compute class weights from `train['target']` and apply them to the training loss: `loss_fn = torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=training_class_weights.to(device))` then `loss = loss_fn(outputs.logits, batch['labels'])`.", "confidence": 0.88, "title": "Class imbalance weights computed from validation set and applied only to validation loss"}, {"body": "In Cell 16, `df_train['text_final'] = (df_train['text_cleaned']).map(str) + ' | ' + (df_train['keyword']).map(str)`. The `keyword` column in the nlp-getting-started dataset has many NaN values (~1/3 of rows). When `str(np.nan)` is called, it produces the string 'nan', which gets concatenated into the text. The tokenizer then treats 'nan' as a regular vocabulary word, introducing spurious tokens into the model input for all samples with missing keywords. Fix: Fill NaN values before mapping: `(df_train['keyword'].fillna('')).map(str)`.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "NaN keyword values produce spurious 'nan' tokens in model input"}, {"body": "In Cell 42, `metric = evaluate.load('accuracy')` creates an accuracy metric before the epoch loop. Inside the loop, `metric.add_batch(...)` is called for each validation batch, and `result = metric.compute()` is called at the end of each epoch. However, `metric.reset()` is never called between epochs. This means the accuracy metric accumulates across epochs: epoch 1's accuracy covers only epoch 1 data, but epoch 2's accuracy covers both epoch 1 and epoch 2 data combined. Fix: Call `metric.reset()` at the start of each epoch's validation loop.", "confidence": 0.92, "title": "Evaluation accuracy metric not reset between epochs - accumulates across epochs"}, {"body": "No `np.random.seed()`, `torch.manual_seed()`, or any random seed is set anywhere in the notebook. Combined with the missing seed in `df_train.sample(frac=1)` (Cell 6), the DataLoader shuffling (Cell 32), and the random weight initialization of the neural network, the entire training procedure is non-deterministic. Results will vary between runs. Fix: Add `random_seed = 42; np.random.seed(random_seed); torch.manual_seed(random_seed); torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(random_seed)` at the start of the notebook.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "No global random seeds anywhere in notebook - non-reproducible training"}]}

