{"findings": [{"body": "In cell 16, `text_final` is constructed by concatenating cleaned text, keyword, AND a file path: `/kaggle/input/nlp-getting-started/train/` for training and `/kaggle/input/nlp-getting-started/test/` for test. When this text is passed through the tokenizer (cell 26), the model can learn to distinguish train vs test samples from the path string alone. This causes data leakage — the validation metrics will be artificially inflated because the model learns split-specific patterns (the word 'train' or 'test' in the path) rather than generalizable tweet features. The path strings should be stripped before tokenization.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Data leakage: Train/test split path embedded in model input features"}, {"body": "In cell 36, `optimizer = AdamW(model.classifier.parameters(), lr=5e-5)` only targets `model.classifier` (the 2-class linear head of DistilBERT) and completely ignores `model.distilbert` (the transformer encoder). This means the pretrained DistilBERT weights are entirely frozen and never updated during the 2-epoch training. For proper fine-tuning of transformer models, all parameters should be trainable. The fix is to use `AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=5e-5)`.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Transformer encoder weights frozen: optimizer only updates classification head"}, {"body": "In cell 42, the code loads `metric = evaluate.load('f1')` (F1 score) but prints it with the dictionary key `'accuracy'`: `{'accuracy': metric.compute(average='weighted')['f1'], ...}`. This is misleading — anyone reading the training output would assume accuracy is being reported when F1 is actually shown. The key should be named 'f1' or 'weighted_f1'.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "F1 score metric mislabeled as 'accuracy' in printed output"}, {"body": "In cell 42, `metric = evaluate.load('f1')` is created once before the epoch loop. Each epoch appends validation predictions via `metric.add_batch()`. The `evaluate` library retains all accumulated predictions in its internal state across `.compute()` calls. Thus epoch 1's F1 score is computed over predictions from both epoch 0 AND epoch 1 combined, not just epoch 1. The metric should be reset between epochs via `metric.reset()` after each `.compute()` call.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "F1 evaluation metric accumulates predictions across all epochs without reset"}, {"body": "The `clean()` function in cell 7 applies numerous `re.sub()` and string methods to text but has no null/None guard. In cell 7, `df_train['text_cleaned'] = df_train['text'].apply(lambda s : clean(s))` calls `clean(s)` which would receive `None` if any text values are NaN, causing an AttributeError when string methods are called on None. The function should either filter out null rows beforehand or check for None at the start and return early.", "confidence": 0.7, "title": "Text cleaning function will crash on null text values"}, {"body": "In cell 13, `remove_emoji()` replaces emojis with a Unicode replacement character (U+FFFD, displayed as �) rather than an empty string. This leaves garbage characters in the text that get passed to the tokenizer, wasting embedding space. The fix is `emoji_pattern.sub('', text)` instead of `emoji_pattern.sub(r'�', text)`.", "confidence": 0.7, "title": "Emoji removal uses Unicode replacement character instead of empty string"}]}


