{"findings": [{"body": "In the SentimentDataset class (Cell 15, line 252/36), the `augment` parameter defaults to `True`. The validation dataset is created at line 275 as `val_dataset = SentimentDataset(val_texts, val_labels)` without specifying `augment=False`. This means the validation dataset receives random BERT MLM-style masking (8% of tokens replaced with [MASK]) during evaluation. Data augmentation should only be applied to training data; validation data must use deterministic preprocessing for accurate and consistent metric computation.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Data augmentation incorrectly applied to validation dataset"}, {"body": "In `WeightedLossTrainer.get_eval_dataloader` (Cell 15, line 318), the evaluation DataLoader is created with `shuffle=True`. Evaluation dataloaders must never shuffle data - this causes non-deterministic evaluation results and violates the principle that accuracy should be deterministic when running model evaluation multiple times without retraining. The Trainer base class defaults to `shuffle=False` for eval dataloaders, so this explicit override introduces incorrect behavior.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Evaluation dataloader uses shuffle=True, causing non-deterministic metrics"}, {"body": "In Cell 17 (test prediction code), `model.train()` is called at line 40 before making predictions on the test set. This puts the model in training mode, activating dropout layers and causing batch normalization to use batch-level statistics instead of running statistics. This results in non-deterministic predictions and degraded inference quality. The code should use `model.eval()` instead.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "model.train() called before test inference instead of model.eval()"}, {"body": "In Cell 17 (line 34), the test dataset is created as `test_dataset = SentimentDataset(test_df['text'], [0]*len(test_df))` using the default `augment=True`. Test inference should use deterministic preprocessing with no random masking. The test set will have 8% of tokens randomly replaced with [MASK] on every access, producing unreliable and non-deterministic predictions.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Data augmentation applied to test inference dataset"}, {"body": "In Cell 17 (line 37), the test DataLoader uses `shuffle=True`, which randomly reorders samples before batching. The resulting `predictions` list will not correspond to the original row order of `test_df['text']`. When `test_df['target'] = predictions` assigns predictions back to the DataFrame, each prediction gets attached to the wrong tweet ID, producing a completely corrupt submission file.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "Test dataloader shuffle causes predictions to be assigned to wrong tweet IDs"}, {"body": "In Cell 15 (lines 340-343), `randomized_label_check()` computes `shuffled_labels = val_labels.sample(frac=1.0, random_state=7).reset_index(drop=True)` but then creates the evaluation dataset with original labels: `SentimentDataset(val_texts.reset_index(drop=True), val_labels.reset_index(drop=True))`. The `shuffled_labels` variable is computed but never used. The function evaluates with original labels instead of randomized ones, so the intended validation check - verifying accuracy drops to random-baseline levels when labels are shuffled - is completely bypassed.", "confidence": 1.0, "title": "randomized_label_check does not actually use shuffled labels"}, {"body": "In `WeightedLossTrainer.compute_loss` (Cell 15, line 309), `torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=class_weights.cuda())` creates a brand new CUDA tensor on every single forward pass. This is inefficient (repeated GPU memory allocation on every batch) and fragile (would fail on CPU since `.cuda()` assumes GPU availability). The weight should be pre-computed outside the method as `class_weights_cuda = class_weights.cuda()` or use `class_weights.to(device)` for device-agnostic correctness.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "CrossEntropyLoss weight tensor created on every forward pass