{"findings": [{"title": "CRITICAL: Data Leakage - Target Label Embedded in Training Text", "body": "Cell 38 appends the actual target label directly into the training text: `train['text_processed'] = train['text_processed'] + \" [target_\" + train['target'].astype(str) + \"]\"`. The RoBERTa model will learn to read the label (0 or 1) directly from the input text rather than learning to classify disaster tweets based on their content. This makes the classification task trivially easy during training but completely useless in production - every training metric is inflated, and submitted predictions would likely perform at chance level on unseen data.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "BUG: Data Augmentation Applied to Test Data", "body": "Cell 40 defines a `tokenize()` function that randomly drops words (35% probability) and appends the word 'storm' (20% probability) using `np.random.rand()`. This augmentation is applied to both the training AND test datasets via `train_dataset.map(tokenize, batched=True)` AND `test_dataset.map(tokenize, batched=True)`. Test predictions will be non-deterministic because different words are randomly removed/added each time the notebook is run. Data augmentation should only be applied to training data.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "BUG: Stochastic Evaluation Forces Training Mode During Inference", "body": "Cell 40 defines `StochasticEvalTrainer` which overrides `prediction_step()` to call `model.train()` before prediction. This forces the model into training mode during evaluation, meaning dropout layers remain active. The evaluation metrics (accuracy, F1, precision, recall) become non-deterministic and artificially inflated. This also affects `trainer.predict(test_dataset)` which internally calls `prediction_step()`, making test predictions non-deterministic as well.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "BUG: Training Dataset Reconstructs Original Data Including Validation Set", "body": "Cell 40 creates a validation split: `split_dataset = train_dataset.train_test_split(test_size=0.2, seed=42)`. But then it concatenates back: `train_dataset_final = concatenate_datasets([split_dataset['train'], split_dataset['test']])`. This makes `train_dataset_final` identical to the original full training dataset. The model trains on ALL data including the 20% used for validation. Combined with `StochasticEvalTrainer`, the model selection based on val_dataset metrics is both on seen data and unreliable.", "confidence": 0.9}, {"title": "BUG: Device Mismatch in Sample Prediction Code", "body": "Cell 40 moves the model to device (GPU or CPU) but in the sample prediction code, input tensors are NOT moved to the device: `model(input_ids=sample['input_ids'].unsqueeze(0), attention_mask=sample['attention_mask'].unsqueeze(0))`. Since `sample` is from `test_dataset` formatted with `set_format('torch')`, the tensors are on CPU. If device is CUDA, this causes a runtime device mismatch error.", "confidence": 0.8}, {"title": "BUG: Warmup Steps Exceed Total Training Steps", "body": "Cell 40 sets `warmup_steps=500` with `num_train_epochs=1` and `per_device_train_batch_size=16`. The disaster tweets dataset has 7875 training samples, giving ceil(7875/16) = 493 steps per epoch. Since `warmup_steps` (500) > steps per epoch (493), the learning rate is still increasing throughout the entire training run and never reaches its peak or begins to decrease, significantly suboptimizing training dynamics.", "confidence": 0.9}, {"title": "BUG: Missing Training Reproducibility (No set_seed)", "body": "Cell 40 sets `seed=42` only for `train_test_split` but never calls `transformers.trainer_utils.set_seed(42)` or equivalent before training. Without setting the random seed, the training loop initialization, DataLoader shuffling, and all random operations during training are non-deterministic. Results differ between runs even with identical data and model.", "confidence": 0.8}, {"title": "BUG: No Class Imbalance Handling Despite Imbalanced Dataset"