, "body": "Cell 0 imports `compute_class_weight` from `sklearn.utils.class_weight`, but it is never used anywhere in the active code. The disaster tweets dataset is imbalanced (~64% non-disaster, ~36% disaster). No class weights are applied to the loss function, no oversampling is used, and no weighted sampling is employed. The model may be biased toward the majority class.", "confidence": 0.7}, {"title": "BUG: compute_metrics Uses Python Loop Instead of Numpy for Argmax", "body": "Cell 40's `compute_metrics` function uses a Python `for` loop to find the best label across rows of predictions. For batched predictions, `np.argmax(p.predictions, axis=1)` would be more efficient. While not a correctness bug, this is an inefficient use of Python control flow where vectorized matrix operations should be used.", "confidence": 0.6}, {"title": "BUG: f1_score Uses Default Binary Average Instead of Specified Type", "body": "Cell 40's `compute_metrics` calls `f1_score(p.label_ids, preds)` without specifying the `average` parameter. For binary classification, sklearn defaults to `average='binary'` with `pos_label=1`, returning F1 for class 1 only. This differs from macro or weighted average F1, which may not match the intended metric if the code author wanted an overall F1 score.", "confidence": 0.5}, {"title": "Minor: Zero-width Space Character Appended to Every Text", "body": "Cell 38's `preprocess_text` returns `text + \"\\u200b\"` (zero-width space, Unicode U+200B) on every processed text. This is a non-printing Unicode character that is not whitespace, so `str.split()` in the `tokenize` function will not separate it. It will be attached to adjacent words, potentially confusing the BERTweet tokenizer and creating unexpected tokens.", "confidence": 0.5}]}


