{"findings": [{"title": "f1_score uses binary averaging by default causing misleading model selection metric", "body": "In the `compute_metrics` function (Cell 40), `f1_score(p.label_ids, preds)` is called without an explicit `average` parameter. For binary classification, f1_score defaults to `average='binary'`, which computes F1 score only for the positive class (class 1). The `metric_for_best_model='f1'` in TrainingArguments then uses this metric for model selection, leading to suboptimal models when the model struggles with one class. For example, if the model predicts all class 0, binary F1=0.0 but macro F1 would be ~0.33, meaning the trainer sees a much worse score than the actual overall performance. Similarly, `precision_score` and `recall_score` also use binary averaging by default. The author should use `f1_score(p.label_ids, preds, average='macro')` for consistent evaluation.", "confidence": 0.95}, {"title": "Train/validation split is not stratified on imbalanced data", "body": "In Cell 40, `train_dataset.train_test_split(test_size=0.2, seed=42)` creates the validation split without stratification. HuggingFace Dataset's `train_test_split` does not stratify by default. The nlp-getting-started dataset is imbalanced (~58% disaster vs ~42% non-disaster), so without stratification the validation set may have a very different class distribution than the training set, leading to unreliable evaluation metrics and suboptimal model selection. The code imports `StratifiedShuffleSplit` in Cell 0 but never uses it. A stratified split should be implemented. Additionally, `random_seed` is not set in TrainingArguments.", "confidence": 0.9}, {"title": "Text preprocessing removes punctuation that bertweet-base tokenizer was trained on", "body": "The `preprocess_text` function in Cell 38 applies `re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z0-9\\s\\[\\]]', '', text)` which strips all punctuation including periods, exclamation marks, question marks. The `vinai/bertweet-base` tokenizer was trained on raw tweets containing punctuation, so stripping it creates a distribution mismatch between pretraining and fine-tuning data, degrading model performance. The tokenizer was designed to handle raw tweet text including punctuation.", "confidence": 0.85}, {"title": "No class imbalance handling despite unused compute_class_weight import", "body": "Cell 0 imports `compute_class_weight` from `sklearn.utils.class_weight` but it is never used. The nlp-getting-started dataset has class imbalance (~58% disaster vs ~42% non-disaster), and without class weights or a weighted loss function, the model will be biased toward the majority class. The code should compute class weights and pass them to the Trainer via a custom loss function.", "confidence": 0.8}, {"title": "Tokenizer pad_token not set for bertweet-base causing potential runtime error", "body": "The bertweet-base tokenizer does not have a pad_token set by default. In Cell 40, the tokenize function calls `tokenizer(batch['text_processed'], padding='max_length', truncation=True, max_length=128)` without first setting `tokenizer.pad_token`. When `padding='max_length'` is used with a tokenizer that lacks a pad_token, this will raise a ValueError at runtime. The fix is to add `tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token` after loading the tokenizer.", "confidence": 0.75}]}


